  "query_preview": "..A closed low off the Baja will gradually weaken and\ndrift northeast into our region through Sunday. A very dry air \nmass is currently in place following a couple days of easterly \nwinds, with precipitable water values ranging from 0.10\" in \nnortheast AZ to around 0.40\" in southern Yavapai County. There \nwill still be some gusty easterly winds over the higher terrain \nand locations just downwind through early this afternoon, but much\nlighter than what was observed Thursday and Friday. As the low \noff Baja moves closer, we will start to see an increase in \nmoisture, with precipitable water values forecast to rise to 0.60\"\nto 0.90\" from about the Mogollon Rim southward by early this \nevening. Forecast soundings indicate this will be a top down \nmoistening process, with the lower levels remaining pretty dry at \nleast initially. This may limit precipitation coverage/intensity \nthis evening into the pre-dawn hours Sunday, before the low levels\nmoisten further and lift from the approaching low brings more \nwidespread showers. Not expecting heavy rainfall out of this \nsystem, with most areas ranging from a few hundredths up to 0.30\",\nand NBM probabilities of over 0.30\" are only 20-40% - focused on \nthe higher terrain of the Mogollon Rim.\n\nMonday through Tuesday - the weekend low moves off to the\nnortheast as another stronger low dives southward just off the\nwest coast. Expecting slightly warmer temperatures with only a\n10-30% chance of showers during this period. From Wednesday\nthrough Friday, the low off the coast is forecast to slowly move\neastward across our region. Still some differences in\nstrength/timing of the system, but still expecting another round\nof showers and a few thunderstorms with the best chances later\nWednesday through Thursday. There is a possibility that this\nsystem could tap some deeper moisture from the south and bring a \nthreat of some heavier rain, but it's too early for specifics. For\nnow, WPC has placed our region in a marginal risk for excessive \nrainfall on Wednesday.",
